WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:05.810
If you read a text or hear something new,
your mind has a strong desire for order and

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:07.490
consistency.

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:16.860
Now, the moment your brain holds two contradicting
ideas, you experience distress.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:22.140
Your mind often resolves this problem, by
tweaking the ideas until they become consistent

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:27.390
with the other stories you tell yourself to
be true.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:31.980
Meat eaters for example who are confronted
with new information of how their dinner came

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:37.860
about can either eat less meat, deploy strategies
to cope with the new information, such as

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:42.739
denial, or do a little bit of both.

00:00:42.739 --> 00:00:48.239
If two conflicting ideas are deeply ingrained
in our identity, the mental imbalance can

00:00:48.239 --> 00:00:54.100
become overwhelming and intoxicate our thoughts
— and as a result we may believe even the

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:57.239
most absurd conspiracy theories.

00:00:57.239 --> 00:01:09.080
To keep our sanity, it helps to learn about
this phenomenon, known as cognitive dissonance.

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:14.259
After a severe earthquake in India, rumors
circulated among people who felt the shock

00:01:14.259 --> 00:01:21.399
but sustained no damage, about an even worse
disaster that was supposed to strike.

00:01:21.399 --> 00:01:28.029
However, no disaster followed, and psychologists
began to wonder why such rumors sprang into

00:01:28.029 --> 00:01:33.780
existence — it seemed counterintuitive that
people would share and believe fear-provoking

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:38.619
stories during such a difficult time.

00:01:38.619 --> 00:01:44.939
Nearly 20 years passed before Leon Festinger,
a young psychologist, had a plausible theory

00:01:44.939 --> 00:01:52.679
: The rumors were not “fear-provoking”
but instead "fear-justifying."

00:01:52.679 --> 00:01:59.349
The argument: if we feel afraid and safe at
the same time, we invent explanations to reduce

00:01:59.349 --> 00:02:02.490
such conflicting states of mind.

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:08.459
Festing called this mental imbalance “cognitive
dissonance”.

00:02:08.459 --> 00:02:13.569
Cognitive dissonance is based on the idea
that when two ideas are psychologically not

00:02:13.569 --> 00:02:20.560
consistent with each other, we change them
and make them consistent.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.569
Festinger then examined whether a cognitive
dissonance can be strong enough to believe

00:02:24.569 --> 00:02:30.870
in conspiracy theories even after they are
proven wrong.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:37.569
To do so, two colleagues and Festinger, joined
a small apocalyptic cult led by Dorothy Martin,

00:02:37.569 --> 00:02:41.370
a suburban housewife.

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:47.810
Martin claimed to receive messages from superior
beings from another planet called 'Clarion'.

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:56.819
The messages purportedly said that a flood
would destroy the world on December 21, 1954.

00:02:56.819 --> 00:03:02.150
The psychologists observed that many members
quit their jobs and let go of their possessions

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:06.969
in preparation for the end of the world.

00:03:06.969 --> 00:03:12.170
When the day came and passed peacefully, Martin
claimed that the world had been spared because

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:17.310
of the "force of good” that the members
had spread.

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:22.540
To cope with this situation and the heavy
cognitive dissonance it produced, the psychologists

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:27.329
hypothesized that the members had two options:

00:03:27.329 --> 00:03:32.019
They could either change their situations,
and let go of the beliefs and actions that

00:03:32.019 --> 00:03:35.359
formed the identity of their former self…

00:03:35.359 --> 00:03:41.120
…or they could embrace new beliefs to stay
in the cult and protect the image they have

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.489
formed of themselves.

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:48.040
And that’s exactly what happened.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:53.010
Some members faced the facts and left the
cult to regain their sanity, while others

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:58.079
believed Martin’s story to keep their threatened
worldview alive.

00:03:58.079 --> 00:04:05.519
In their assessment, the psychologist concluded
that 3 conditions made it especially hard

00:04:05.519 --> 00:04:08.230
for members to quit:

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:09.930
1.

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:14.779
If the members had deep convictions that were
relevant to their everyday routines.

00:04:14.779 --> 00:04:15.959
2.

00:04:15.959 --> 00:04:19.180
If they had done things that were difficult
to undo.

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:20.220
3.

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:25.840
If they received social support from other
believers.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:31.389
Leon Festinger later wrote.“A man with a
conviction is a hard man to change.

00:04:31.389 --> 00:04:34.040
Tell him you disagree and he turns away.

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:37.210
Show him facts and he questions your sources.

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:42.900
Appeal to logic and he fails to see your point.”

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:44.300
What are your thoughts?

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:48.360
Do you have an idea how we can change ours
and other people's minds?

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:54.150
If reason falls on deaf ears, can experience
maybe do the trick?

00:04:54.150 --> 00:05:14.110
Share your thoughts and experience with cognitive
dissonance in the comments below!

